{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-42041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4z=2 \\\\ 4x+y-5z=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+2z \\\\ y=-3z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2z \\\\ 4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1+4z \\\\ 3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2+4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2+4z \\\\ 1-3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+8y=-6 \\\\  & -4x-16y=12 \\\\  & 6x+24y=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4y-3 \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4y+3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-5{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}+4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131717" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y+2z=2 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=0 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+2y=5 \\\\  & -10x-10y=-25 \\\\  & 8x+8y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-32244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}+x \\\\ x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5}-2 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=2 \\\\  & -3x-4y=3 \\\\  & 2x-4y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is inconsistent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-5,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y=-1 \\\\  & 3x+y=3 \\\\  & -2x+y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is inconsistent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=2,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-42020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x+8y=-2 \\\\  & -3x-12y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x-3y=6 \\\\  & -2x+y=-3 \\\\  & 4x-4y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system has no solution" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=-1$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=1.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y+z=0 \\\\  & -4x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we can to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-42061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-42061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+7y=6 \\\\  & x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & k & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -3 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2k+7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=-3 \\\\  & (2k+7)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=-\\frac{7}{2},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=-3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=-\\frac{7}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-7"] ["math" "k=-\\frac{2}{7}"] ["math" "k=-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (4-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-4"] ["math" "k=-\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "k=\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-32246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y=-1 \\\\  & 4x+3ky=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3k & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3k & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3k+12 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=-1 \\\\ (3k+12)y=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=7,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-12"] ["math" "k=-13"] ["math" "k=-3"] ["math" "k=-4"] ["math" "k=7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-74646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=2 \\\\  & 2x+(k+1)y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-74646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=2 \\\\ (k-7)y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-74646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-74646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-74646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=1"] ["math" "k=3"] ["math" "k=5"] ["math" "k=7"] ["math" "k=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-74646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+6y=-4 \\\\  & 5x+(k-6)y=10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k-6 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k-6 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & k+9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+6y=-4 \\\\  & (k+9)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=-9,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $-2x+6y=-4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=-9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bbf8c84288812bc42555f6b12911137b4fe0e0cf7c142f3b33edf7624d0a64c3"} {"question" "q-131731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-12"] ["math" "k=-6"] ["math" "k=-9"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "q-131731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac4128259c0f5d88a62da5d7367975f113125fa5ec159e55efea36ea09dbadbe"} {"question" "e-4711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4711.json" "file_sha256" "e2adb0de8abfc6de2c8095a2b427d0653155ac6ceb41323ad57fc50d10a2b847"} {"question" "e-4711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4711.json" "file_sha256" "e2adb0de8abfc6de2c8095a2b427d0653155ac6ceb41323ad57fc50d10a2b847"} {"question" "e-6173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/5f1aaf29a3dd8b7ea2c4f7104d532f7b56f32a23ccebbcfa0d1bfe86c772cc5f.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-6173.json" "file_sha256" "069b62aacb001f4e3286f0b3ffd7c22afdf4f8de90f5ea3fbb6c4c79135e894b"} {"question" "e-6173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/6171d242b0a3887802f629de79cb952d47711a581e88db2d9304a5db356dfb62.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/80aff0fd62e3e1aeed17017c864d8d0b875b556ce1a29a89e6c61e632bf9c733.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-6173.json" "file_sha256" "069b62aacb001f4e3286f0b3ffd7c22afdf4f8de90f5ea3fbb6c4c79135e894b"} {"question" "e-4712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4712.json" "file_sha256" "31a00ffb86ea7799eb53128b8d45d4ee583fa2d9f3b626e49737b32b6836892a"} {"question" "e-4712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a115d3da691619918bf92d914acdfdd95088e9a141a82ca3fe3230fa7fbb8172.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/example-4712.json" "file_sha256" "31a00ffb86ea7799eb53128b8d45d4ee583fa2d9f3b626e49737b32b6836892a"}] "usage" {"q-42061" [] "e-6173" [] "e-4712" [] "e-4711" [] "q-32246" []} "basis" 1575 "retractions" [[17592186115743 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{rl} & x - 3 y + z = 0 \\\\  & - 4 x + 6 y = 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The system is consistent dependent\n2. The system has no solution\n3. The system is consistent independent"] [17592186115672 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{rl} & 3 x + 4 y = 12 \\\\  & x + k y = 4\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186114987 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\t\\begin{array}{rl} & x + 5 y + z = 2 \\\\  & x + 5 y + 2 z = 5.\\end{array}\n\t\\right.\n$$\n\n.\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186114987 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{array}\n\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rlrl}M & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & := {R}_{2} + ( - 1 ) {R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & := {R}_{1} + ( - 1 ) {R}_{2} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{array}$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{rl} & x + 5 y = - 1 \\\\  & z = 3.\\end{array}\n\\right.$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 1 - 5 y \\\\ y = y \\\\ z = 3 ,\\end{array} \\right.$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 - 5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, y \\in  (- \\infty , \\infty )\n$$"] [17592186115041 :question/problem "Given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\t\\begin{array}{rl} & 2 x - 3 y = 4 \\\\  & - 4 x + 6 y = - 8 ,\\end{array}\n\t\\right.\n$$\n\n,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The system is consistent dependent\n2. The system has no solution\n3. The system is consistent independent"] [17592186115041 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 3 & 4 \\\\ - 4 & 6 & - 8\\end{array}\n\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rlrl}M & = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 3 & 4 \\\\ - 4 & 6 & - 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & := {R}_{2} + 2 {R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{array}$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\\left\\{\n\t\\begin{array}{rl} & 2 x - 3 y = 4 \\\\  & 0 x + 0 y = 0.\\end{array}\n\t\\right.$$\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y=0\n$$\n\nwe can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x$, and we have a free variable $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."] [17592186114988 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = 1 , \\\\ 4 x + k y = - 3\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186114988 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$M=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & - 3\\end{array}\n\\right].$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rlrl}M & = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & - 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & := {R}_{2} - 2 {R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k + 4 & - 5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{array}$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{rl} & 2 x - 2 y = 1 \\\\  & ( k + 4 ) y = - 5.\\end{array}\n\\right.$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4$, then the second equation is $0=-5$, which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113433 17592186113434 17592186113435 17592186113436 17592186113437 17592186113830 17592186113831 17592186113832 17592186113833 17592186113834] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113432 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113432 :answer-field/id] [17592186113432 :answer-field/key] [17592186113432 :answer-field/type] [17592186113829 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113829 :answer-field/id] [17592186113829 :answer-field/key] [17592186113829 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
